---
import WorkLayout from '../../layouts/WorkLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import ReportBuilder from '../../components/sketches/ReportBuilder.astro';

const description = "Strive Studio: vibe-coding for live marketing dashboards. An agent that knows your business, your data, and your brand writes the whole report as live software, keeps it in sync, and renders it in the browser. Frontend built by Mariappan Subramanian.";
---
<WorkLayout
  title="Strive Studio — agent-written live reports"
  description={description}
  ogDescription="Vibe-coding for live marketing dashboards. An agent writes the whole report as live software that pulls warehouse data, renders in your brand, and stays in sync."
  eyebrow="Strivelabs · Founding engineer · 2025 → now"
  heading="Strive Studio"
  facts={[
    { label: 'Role', value: 'Founding engineer · frontend + prompts' },
    { label: 'Scope', value: 'Frontend from zero' },
    { label: 'Status', value: 'In production, still shipping' },
  ]}
  link={{ href: 'https://strivelabs.ai/', label: 'strivelabs.ai' }}
  schema={{
    name: 'Strive Studio — report builder',
    url: 'https://strivelabs.ai/',
    description: "Vibe-coding for live marketing dashboards: an agent writes the whole report as live software that pulls warehouse data, renders in the customer's brand, and stays in sync.",
    inLanguage: 'en',
    keywords: 'generative UI, AI reports, live dashboards, sandboxed iframe, postMessage, BigQuery, brand theming, design engineering',
  }}
>
  <ReportBuilder slot="sketch" />

  <Fragment slot="lede">Vibe-coding for live dashboards — <mark class="hl">Lovable for marketing</mark>. Describe the report you want; an agent that knows your business, your data, and your brand writes it, keeps it live, and holds it in sync.</Fragment>

  <h2>The setup</h2>
  <ul class="doodle-list">
    <li>Building reports and dashboards is one of a marketer's most constant jobs — and it's still done the hard way, stitched across fragmented tools.</li>
    <li>Vibe-code a deck in Claude today and it looks great for a week — then the data's stale, and keeping it current is manual work all over again.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What we built</h2>
  <ul class="doodle-list">
    <li>Connect your data sources, ask for what you want, and get a rich, live dashboard — shareable, collaborative, always in sync.</li>
    <li>It's personal, not generic vibe-coding: it reads your business context, runs against your data, and renders in your brand — so a report looks like your team made it, not ours.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The bet we made</h2>
  <ul class="doodle-list">
    <li>We could've kept a JSON-contract UI — strict schemas, tight control, every component defined by us.</li>
    <li>Instead we bet the agent could be trained on our design system: write its own code and components, stay true to our design principles, and skip the schema wrangling. More trust in the model, far more flexibility.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Live artifacts, early</h2>
  <ul class="doodle-list">
    <li>The building block is a <em>slot</em> — an analysis you can render, schedule, and export.</li>
    <li>When Claude shipped static artifacts, we already had live ones in production with clients: full-stack dashboards they could vibe-code against their own data.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where it got hard</h2>
  <ul class="doodle-list">
    <li>Reports render live in the browser, against big datasets and live external sources. Done naively, that's a frozen tab — we saw our share of crashes early on.</li>
    <li>So we trained the agent to fetch data <em>lazily</em>, only when it's needed, and to compose dashboards that stay smooth: efficient reads and writes, caching that also keeps token use in check.</li>
    <li>Sensitive data never reaches the browser in the clear — queries run through a secured sandboxed bridge, so a report can <em>ask</em> for data without ever holding the keys.</li>
  </ul>
  <!-- .flow is white-space: pre, so the line breaks below are the layout. -->
  <div class="flow" role="img" aria-label="Data path: the sandboxed report asks the host over postMessage, the host queries the Cube semantic layer over BigQuery, and results travel back into the sandbox to render.">
<b class="bad">SANDBOX</b>   agent-written HTML ─▶ asks over postMessage
<b class="blue">HOST</b>      TanStack Query ─▶ Cube ─▶ BigQuery
<b class="good">BACK</b>      results ─▶ through the bridge ─▶ charts render</div>
  <p>It's a coordinated effort — the team holds the secured bridge on the backend, and I built and maintain the frontend side of the loop: auth, retries, loading, and keeping the sandbox in sync on every read and write.</p>

  <h2>Under the hood</h2>
  <p>React 19 · TypeScript · TanStack Query · agent-authored HTML in a sandboxed iframe · Cube + BigQuery · lazy, cached live data. Full teardown coming to the blog.</p>

  <!--
    Adding media: drop files into public/images/work/report-builder/ and use one of these.

    <figure>
      <img src="/images/work/report-builder/canvas.png" alt="Describe what the image shows" width="1600" height="1000" loading="lazy">
      <figcaption>A report being rewritten from the conversation beside it</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure>
      <video src="/images/work/report-builder/build.mp4" poster="/images/work/report-builder/build-poster.jpg" controls playsinline preload="none" width="1600" height="900"></video>
      <figcaption>From a sentence to a branded, live report</figcaption>
    </figure>
  -->

  <h2>What stuck with me</h2>
  <ul class="doodle-list">
    <li>Trained well on a design system, an agent writing its own components beat us hand-authoring UI contracts.</li>
  </ul>

  <blockquote>A dashboard shows you numbers someone else chose. This builds the report you asked for — and keeps it alive.</blockquote>

  <aside class="next-up callout wobbly wobbly--2" aria-label="Upcoming write-up">
    <Icon name="pen" />
    <div>
      <strong>Deep dive: multi-page PDF export from a sandboxed report</strong>
      <p>Self-snapshotting the DOM, pagination that respects charts, and the Lambda pipeline behind it. <span class="soon">Blog landing here soon.</span></p>
    </div>
  </aside>

  <aside class="next-up callout wobbly wobbly--3" aria-label="Upcoming write-up">
    <Icon name="pen" />
    <div>
      <strong>Deep dive: extracting a design system from a domain</strong>
      <p>How brand tokens get pulled at onboarding and fed to the agent as generation context. <span class="soon">Blog landing here soon.</span></p>
    </div>
  </aside>
</WorkLayout>
